# clusters/@@CLUSTER@@/namespaces/@@NAMESPACE@@/30-netpol-developer-team-egress.yaml
#
# The per-tier DEFAULT-DENY egress allowlist for @@NAMESPACE@@'s Developer Team Agent pod (03 §10; Phase 3).
# It layers on top of `20-netpol-default-deny.yaml`: the baseline denies all egress for every pod in
# @@NAMESPACE@@; this policy adds back — for the agent pod ONLY (selected by the tier label the controller
# stamps, `kube-agents/tier: developer-team`) — exactly the destinations the agent needs. Tenant
# workloads are unaffected (different/no selector) and stay default-denied until they add their own
# policy. SHAPE = PURE ALLOWLIST: there is deliberately NO 0.0.0.0/0 rule, so the cloud metadata server
# (169.254.169.254), other team namespaces, and arbitrary hosts are unreachable by omission (03 §11).
#
# A4 GRAFT — why an in-cluster hop to kubeagents-system (load-bearing). The controller renders the
# agent's config with hostnames hard-coded to the POD's OWN namespace:
#     model.baseURL      = http://litellm.@@NAMESPACE@@.svc.cluster.local/v1              (agent_manifests.go)
#     GITHUB_TOKEN_URL   = http://github-token-minter.@@NAMESPACE@@.svc.cluster.local:8080/token
# Those names don't exist in @@NAMESPACE@@, so `40-service-aliases.yaml` creates ExternalName aliases
# `litellm`/`github-token-minter` in @@NAMESPACE@@ that point at the shared services in kubeagents-system. The
# agent thus consumes the CLUSTER's inference + token-minter (it gets no hub connection of its own).
# This policy must therefore admit that hop — rule (2) allows egress to the kubeagents-system namespace
# on the litellm (80) and minter (8080) ports. On Kind (the inner-loop verification target) those are
# real in-cluster services, so rule (2) is the working path. In production kubeagents-system may itself
# front the hub via ExternalName; rules (3)/(4) then cover the resolved hub CIDR. Both are present so the
# policy is correct under either topology.
#
# EXTERNAL CIDRS (rules 3–6) are REPLACE_WITH_* placeholders substituted per environment by the
# propose-developer-team cascade (mirrors the @@CLUSTER@@dmin egress policy). They are validated
# structurally in the Kind gate (policy parses, selects the pod by tier label, zero 0.0.0.0/0);
# enforcement of the isolation negatives is proven on a Calico cluster (see verify-phase3).
#
# LIMITATION (hostname precision) & GKE Workload-Identity metadata note: identical to the @@CLUSTER@@dmin
# egress policy — NetworkPolicy is L3/L4 (CIDRs drift; the hostname-precise L7 proxy is Phase 5), and
# WI token fetches via 169.254.169.254 are intentionally NOT allowed (the load-bearing negative is that
# raw metadata / node credentials are unreachable; on Kind there is no metadata server).
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: developer-team-egress
  namespace: @@NAMESPACE@@
  labels:
    kube-agents/tier: developer-team
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: gitops
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      kube-agents/tier: developer-team
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    # 1) DNS — cluster resolution (and the ExternalName CNAME chain in rule 2). Without this, every
    #    name-based egress fails before it starts.
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53
    # 2) Shared cluster services in kubeagents-system — the A4 alias targets. Inference (LiteLLM, :80)
    #    and the GitHub/Workload-Identity token broker (github-token-minter, :8080). The agent reaches
    #    these via the @@NAMESPACE@@ ExternalName aliases; this is the ONLY cross-namespace egress it gets, and
    #    it is one-way to the cluster's own control namespace — never to another tenant.
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kubeagents-system
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 80
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8080
    # 3) Hub Inference (LiteLLM) — used when kubeagents-system fronts the hub via ExternalName, so the
    #    resolved connection lands on the hub's VPC-internal private endpoint. TCP 443.
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: REPLACE_WITH_HUB_INFERENCE_CIDR # e.g. 10.10.0.0/28 — the hub LiteLLM private endpoint
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
    # 4) Hub Minty — the Workload-Identity / GitHub token broker, when fronted from the hub. TCP 443.
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: REPLACE_WITH_HUB_MINTY_CIDR # e.g. 10.10.0.16/28 — the hub Minty private endpoint
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
    # 5) Google cloud APIs via Private Google Access (restricted.googleapis.com). Stable range — reads
    #    only (the agent's cloud GSA is viewer-only; the write path is a GitOps PR).
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 199.36.153.8/30
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
    # 6) GitHub — the agent pushes suggestion branches / opens PRs (submit-suggestion) with a Minty-
    #    minted token. Allowlisted by GitHub's published IP ranges (https://api.github.com/meta).
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: REPLACE_WITH_GITHUB_CIDRS # e.g. 140.82.112.0/20 (see api.github.com/meta)
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
    # 7) MCP grounding endpoints the agent reads live docs from (developer_knowledge — 03 §10).
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: REPLACE_WITH_MCP_GROUNDING_CIDRS # e.g. the hub MCP endpoint CIDR
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 443
